<h2>Managing Usage · Context · Sessions</h2>
<p>To use Claude Code well, you need to know how to manage <strong>usage (tokens)</strong>, <strong>context</strong>, and <strong>sessions (conversational state)</strong>. Tokens have become extremely important.</p>

<h3>Usage — /usage</h3>
<ul>
    <li><code>/usage</code> — shows your current session and this week's usage.</li>
    <li><strong>Session limit</strong>: budgeted in <strong>5-hour</strong> blocks counted from when you first started. If you burn through 100% within those 5 hours, you have to wait until the block ends.</li>
    <li>There is also a <strong>weekly limit</strong>. It is the limit developers find most painful, so spending <strong>frugally</strong> is the key.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Context — /context</h3>
<p><code>/context</code> visualizes what is currently loaded into Claude Code. The size of the context window <strong>depends on the model</strong> — most default to 200,000 (200K) tokens, while the latest models (Fable 5 · Sonnet 5 · Sonnet 4.6, and Opus 4.6 onward) support <strong>1,000,000 (1M) tokens</strong>. On the Max · Team · Enterprise plans Opus is automatically upgraded to the 1M context, and you can also pick it yourself by adding the <code>[1m]</code> suffix, as in <code>/model opus[1m]</code>.</p>
<ul>
    <li>The system prompt, tool definitions, and the like take up part of it, and it fills up a little more with every task you assign.</li>
    <li>Once it is fairly full, a <strong>compact</strong> kicks in and the contents are compressed and shrunk.</li>
    <li>When a compact happens, <strong>some data is lost</strong>, so performance can drop slightly.</li>
    <li>So rather than waiting until it is completely full, it is recommended to <strong>compact manually</strong> with <code>/compact</code> at a sensible point.</li>
</ul>
<p>Making use of agents lets you conserve the main context while keeping quality up.</p>

<h3>Managing sessions (conversational state)</h3>
<p>Unlike a web chat, Claude Code loses its context once you leave with <code>/exit</code>. Use the following commands to pick it back up.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>claude --continue</code></td><td>Resumes the most recent session exactly where it left off</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>claude --resume</code></td><td>Pick from a list of past sessions and return to that context</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/clear</code></td><td>Clears the context without exiting and starts a new session</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>Session records are saved per project and per session <strong>under your user folder in <code>~/.claude/projects/</code></strong>. That means even work from long ago can be revived with <code>--resume</code>. Note, however, that these records are stored <strong>locally only</strong>, so they do not exist on another PC.</p>

<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">Watch out for caching</span>
    AI has caching, so when you continue a request you are billed only for the new portion. But caching has a <strong>time to live</strong>, so if you come back later and request again with <code>--resume</code>, everything accumulated is sent over again and you get no caching benefit. In that case, opening a <strong>new session</strong> may work out better.
</div>
